WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:10.470
- Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.360
Thank you so much for
coming out this afternoon.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:17.383
And so welcome, all of you, to
the Chubb Fellowship lecture

00:00:17.383 --> 00:00:21.940
a very distinguished guest,
Dr. Hawa Abdi Dhiblawe.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:25.177
As Somalia's most-- (applauding)

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:32.160
Somalia's most celebrated
and known physician,

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.800
humanitarian and human rights activist.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.130
And we're also extremely
delighted to have Dr. Abdi

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.773
and her daughter, Dr. Deqo Mohamed.

00:00:41.739 --> 00:00:44.156
(applauding)

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:50.600
A similarly important physician
and human rights activist.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.670
We're really thrilled to
have both of them back,

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:55.940
well, for Deqo, back at Yale with us,

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:59.500
and for Dr. Abdi, of course,
the first time to Yale.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.490
This is truly an honor.

00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:03.980
So I wanna begin by first
asking everyone to please

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:07.660
turn off their cellphones
or any kinds of devices

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:10.400
that you might have, just
so that we don't disrupt

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.003
the conversation today.

00:01:15.858 --> 00:01:17.600
And we also ask that if
you are taking photographs,

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.710
to probably refrain from doing
so, especially with the flash

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:22.060
because it's very bright in here,

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:24.429
and it's already quite distracting.

00:01:24.429 --> 00:01:26.820
So that would be very, very helpful.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:28.830
So I should pause and introduce myself.

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:32.360
I'm Mary Lui, I'm the head
of Timothy Dwight College.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.470
I'm a professor in the
Department of History,

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.410
in the program in American Studies.

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:39.520
As the head of Timothy Dwight College,

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.540
I have the honor of serving as custodian

00:01:42.385 --> 00:01:43.960
of the illustrious Chubb Fellowship.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:47.930
Established in 1941 out
of a prior large donation

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:49.840
for education purposes.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:53.210
Made in 1936 by Hendon
Chubb, who graduated

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:56.140
from the class of 1895.

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:58.540
Since establishment, the
Chubb Fund has adhered

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:00.710
to the goals of providing encouragement

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:03.940
and aid to Yale students
interested in government

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:05.870
and American public affairs.

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:07.680
And I'm pleased to announce this year

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.020
that we took some of the
Chubb Fellowship money

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:14.166
and created summer
fellowships for TD students

00:02:14.166 --> 00:02:16.210
to engage in the world of public service.

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:17.620
And it's really thrilling.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:20.330
We've awarded six Chubb
fellowships this year,

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:22.550
and it's extremely exciting to see

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:23.950
what these students will do.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:28.080
The fellowship initially
aimed to foster among Yale

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.030
undergraduates an
interest in public service

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:32.212
and local and state affairs.

00:02:32.212 --> 00:02:33.340
It grew over the years to include numerous

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:36.700
distinguished visitors in
national and international affairs

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.653
as well as leaders in the
world of arts and humanities.

00:02:40.582 --> 00:02:43.905
Since the 1940's, the Chubb
Fellows lecture series

00:02:43.905 --> 00:02:46.893
has inspired generations of
Yale students to undertake

00:02:46.893 --> 00:02:48.870
public service and pursue leadership roles

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:51.450
in the hopes of using their Yale education

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:55.800
to create a better world
today and for posterity.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.660
The fellowship has hosted
four US presidents,

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:03.560
George HW Bush, Gerald Ford,
Jimmy Carter, and Harry Truman.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:05.870
And in recent years, we
have welcomed a wide range

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:08.280
of national and international leaders,

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:11.440
including Wynton Marsalis,
Aung San Suu Kyi,

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:12.733
and Shah Rukh Khan.

00:03:14.009 --> 00:03:16.632
It's a great honor for
Timothy Dwight College

00:03:16.632 --> 00:03:18.400
to offer this public lectureship series

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:21.160
as a gift to the Yale University community

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:24.200
as well as the greater
New Haven community.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:27.163
So welcome to all of
you to today's events.

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:30.300
So with this quick
description of the history

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.950
and aims of the fellowship, I'm delighted

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:36.320
that we're welcoming to
Yale today Dr. Hawa Abdi.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:38.560
We're most grateful to her and her family

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:42.240
for working with us since
early January to join us

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:45.770
in New Haven, and to be
honest, when we began

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:48.950
the conversations, we
did not expect for this

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:52.040
spring semester's Chubb Fellowship lecture

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:54.530
that we would be navigating
such a complicated

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:57.430
and difficult legal and political terrain

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:00.600
to bring Dr. Abdi into the United States.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:04.060
But we're extremely thankful
that she is here with us today.

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:06.750
And I want to take a moment
to acknowledge the many people

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:11.180
on this campus, that is, here
on this campus and beyond,

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:13.330
who were very steadfast
in their assistance

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:15.350
in bringing Dr. Abdi here.

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:17.620
First and foremost, I
wish to particularly thank

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:20.813
President Peter Salovey, and
his office, for giving support.

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:25.080
Yale University's Office
of the General Counsel,

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:28.710
particularly Alex Dreier, vice
president and general counsel,

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:30.820
and his wonderful staff of attorneys,

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:33.990
Kara Haas, Caroline
Hendel, and Susan Sawyer,

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:37.460
and I just want the three of
them to pop up for a second

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:40.249
and just be acknowledged
for all their amazing work.

00:04:40.249 --> 00:04:42.666
(applauding)

00:04:46.425 --> 00:04:48.680
I think I owe you all some flowers.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:51.430
I wish to thank my colleagues
also in the Yale Law School,

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:53.790
particularly Professor
Muneer Ahmad, for his advice

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:56.870
and support, and last but
not least, I wish to thank

00:04:56.870 --> 00:05:00.467
Assistant Dean Sara
Lulo, who's right there.

00:05:00.467 --> 00:05:02.884
(applauding)

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:07.340
Who directs the law school's
international programs

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:10.293
and Gruber Program for Global
Justice and Women's Rights.

00:05:11.229 --> 00:05:13.060
Dean Lulo's ongoing support
throughout the spring

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:15.560
has been instrumental in today's events,

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:19.580
and I cannot thank her enough
for everything she's done.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:24.580
So I will pause here and
introduce two wonderful people,

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:28.610
an alum and a current
student, to introduce our

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:31.770
distinguished guests for this afternoon.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:34.030
Introducing Dr. Abdi
and Dr. Mohamed will be

00:05:34.030 --> 00:05:37.590
Nicola Suku, who graduated from Yale,

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:39.970
and Timothy Dwight College, in 2016,

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:42.130
and senior Charlotte Finegold.

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:44.740
I've asked these amazing
students to do the honor

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:46.680
or introducing our distinguished guests,

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:49.120
because it was Nicola's first efforts

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:52.010
that brought the incredible
work of Doctors Abdi and Mohamed

00:05:52.010 --> 00:05:54.180
to my attention, and I
can't thank her enough

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:58.070
for reaching out and telling
me about their amazing work.

00:05:58.070 --> 00:06:00.670
So Nicola's originally from
Cape Town, South Africa,

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:02.930
and majored in Ethics,
Politics, and Economics

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:06.310
and participated in the
multidisciplinary academic program

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:09.620
in education studies; as
a senior, she co-directed

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:12.600
the project to design human
rights conflict resolution

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:16.450
curriculum for the Dr.
Hawa Abdi Foundation.

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:18.910
After graduating, she
moved back to South Africa

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:22.340
to work at Equal Education,
a youth-led social movement

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:25.520
that uses research,
litigation, and activism

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:28.730
to fight systemic inequalities
in the South African

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:29.810
education system.

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:32.940
So we're thrilled to have
her back from South Africa,

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:35.251
to be here with us today.

00:06:35.251 --> 00:06:36.810
And Charlotte is from California,

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:39.850
and a member of the first
class of the multidisciplinary

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:43.600
program in human rights
connected to the Yale Law School.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:46.050
She now directs a team of undergraduates,

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:48.190
who are designing the
Hope Village Human Rights

00:06:48.190 --> 00:06:50.100
and Conflict Resolution Curriculum

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:52.370
for the Dr. Hawa Abdi Foundation.

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:54.310
Please join me in welcoming Nicola

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:56.339
and Charlotte to the podium.

00:06:56.339 --> 00:06:58.756
(applauding)

00:07:09.872 --> 00:07:11.070
- Thank you, Professor Liu.

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:13.750
We are very grateful to you
and the Chubb Fellowship

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:16.350
for giving us the
opportunity to introduce two

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:18.780
of our personal heroes, Dr. Hawa Abdi

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:20.370
and her daughter, Dr. Deqo Mohamed,

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:21.970
for this very prestigious event.

00:07:23.924 --> 00:07:26.470
- Okay, Dr. Abdi was born
in Mogadishu, Somalia.

00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:29.390
She studied medicine on a
Soviet Union scholarship,

00:07:29.390 --> 00:07:32.480
and became one of her
country's first gynecologists.

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:35.950
While practicing as a doctor,
Dr. Abdi also obtained

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:37.370
her law degree.

00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:40.380
Later, she noticed the need
for a rural women's clinic

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:42.820
and established one of
those on her family's land,

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:44.490
close to Mogadishu.

00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:48.419
For years, she woke up very
early to tend to her farm,

00:07:48.419 --> 00:07:49.730
and then traveled to
Mogadishu, where she worked

00:07:49.730 --> 00:07:53.770
full time as a doctor and
professor of medicine.

00:07:53.770 --> 00:07:56.910
And then afterwards, in
the evenings, returned home

00:07:56.910 --> 00:08:00.440
to see to the line of women
waiting outside her clinic,

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:01.303
on her land.

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:04.720
These women paid only
if they had the means,

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:06.990
and Dr. Abdi invested all that she had

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:10.530
into maintaining and growing
the clinic to meet the need.

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:12.720
But following the outbreak
of civil war in Somalia

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:16.700
in 1991, the need became overwhelming.

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:19.320
As those fleeing the
violence of Mogadishu by foot

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:23.370
arrived at the clinic en masse,
Dr. Abdi opened up her land

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:26.020
to provide them with medical aid, food,

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:27.593
clean water, and shelter.

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:31.620
She stretched her limited
resources as far as she could,

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:34.349
in an attempt to save the
lives of the hundreds of people

00:08:34.349 --> 00:08:36.470
who arrived at the camp, daily.

00:08:36.470 --> 00:08:39.120
But in the first year of the
war, Dr. Abdi and her team

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:41.533
were forced to bury
more than 10,000 people.

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:45.440
Nevertheless, she persevered.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:47.370
Renamed the Hawa Abdi Hope Village,

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:50.340
the site is now home
to over 90,000 people,

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:52.520
who live peacefully together.

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:54.850
They have access to a 400-bed hospital,

00:08:54.850 --> 00:08:56.890
a sustainable agricultural program,

00:08:56.890 --> 00:08:59.993
a women's education center,
and primary and high schools.

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:04.150
Dr. Abdi's 2013 memoir,
Keeping Hope Alive,

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:06.400
co-written with Sarah Robbins,
who has joined us here

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:10.870
today, details a life
of remarkable strength.

00:09:10.870 --> 00:09:14.450
Even as the violence of
war, famine, and poverty

00:09:14.450 --> 00:09:17.740
took the lives of so many,
including some of those

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:18.773
most dearest to her.

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:22.140
Dr. Abdi's resilience
and the resilience of all

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:25.389
she has built, is evident still today.

00:09:25.389 --> 00:09:27.777
In Somalia, where there's
yet another terrible drought,

00:09:27.777 --> 00:09:31.063
her village is mobilizing to
feed and care for its people.

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:35.424
Dr. Abdi has been recognized
in the US an abroad

00:09:35.424 --> 00:09:37.720
for her decades of
incredible humanitarian work.

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:40.220
She is a Nobel Peace Prize nominee,

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:42.760
and has received the
John J. Justice Award,

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:45.370
the Vital Voices Woman of the Year Award,

00:09:45.370 --> 00:09:48.140
BET's Social Humanitarian Award,

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:51.660
and the Roosevelt Institute's
Freedom From Want Award.

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:53.290
And it is fitting that
she should be honored

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:54.790
with a Chubb Fellowship today.

00:09:56.470 --> 00:10:00.160
- Dr. Deqo Mohamed and her
sister, Dr. Amina Mohamed,

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:03.000
have always shared their
mother's vision for Hope Village

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:06.390
and have assisted in the hospital
since they were teenagers.

00:10:06.390 --> 00:10:09.270
Dr. Deqo Mohamed studied
medicine in Moscow.

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:11.670
She then came to the US as a refugee,

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:14.160
where she gained extensive
experience in health care,

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:16.040
before returning to Somalia.

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:18.340
She now runs Hope Village and is CEO

00:10:18.340 --> 00:10:20.470
of the Dr. Hawa Abdi Foundation.

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:22.920
She also advises the
Somalian Ministry of Health

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:24.790
on women's and girls' rights.

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:26.090
Her sister now lives in Kenya,

00:10:26.090 --> 00:10:28.570
where she runs her own
women's health clinic.

00:10:28.570 --> 00:10:29.920
The partnership between Yale

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:33.150
and the Dr. Hawa Abdi Foundation
began several years ago,

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:36.350
and strengthened last fall
when Dr. Deqo Mohamed was here

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:39.010
as a Greenberg World
Fellow, and teamed up with

00:10:39.010 --> 00:10:41.540
Gabriella Hernandez
Cardoso, another member

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:43.820
of the World Fellows class of 2016,

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:46.570
and Professor James Levinson
of the Jackson Institute

00:10:46.570 --> 00:10:49.100
for Global Affairs, to
design a course in which

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:52.480
Yale students would create a
business plan for Hope Village.

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:55.070
This spring, graduate and
undergraduate students

00:10:55.070 --> 00:10:57.910
from across Yale are helping
Hope Village residents

00:10:57.910 --> 00:11:00.210
to identify products
that they can manufacture

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:03.450
and sell both inside and
outside of Hope Village,

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:06.330
so as to make the
community more sustainable.

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:08.390
And Nicola and I are endlessly thankful

00:11:08.390 --> 00:11:11.140
to have been entrusted
with another project that,

00:11:11.140 --> 00:11:13.440
had Liu mentioned, that Dr.
Deqo Mohamed commissioned

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:16.580
from Yale: a human rights and
conflict resolution curriculum

00:11:16.580 --> 00:11:18.680
for Hope Village's new high school.

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:21.680
Since October, 2015, when
our team began working

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:24.150
on this task, we have
had guidance and support

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:27.360
from Dr. Deqo Mohamed and
her teachers on the ground,

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:29.630
as well as from Hope
Metcalf and Jim Silke,

00:11:29.630 --> 00:11:32.935
the directors of the Schell
Center for International

00:11:32.935 --> 00:11:34.110
Human Rights, and Sarah Luloe.

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:36.570
Our four-year curriculum,
rooted in the history

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:38.810
and values of Hope
Village, will get students

00:11:38.810 --> 00:11:40.790
to think critically about
the history of conflict

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:43.140
in Somalia, about what human rights are

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:45.120
and how they can use them
to advocate for change

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:47.070
in their community, and we are so proud

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:50.684
of the hard work put in by our
team, who is also here today.

00:11:50.684 --> 00:11:54.190
- We feel incredibly lucky
to have Dr. Hawa Abdi

00:11:54.190 --> 00:11:56.920
with us today, to share
lessons from a lifetime

00:11:56.920 --> 00:12:00.020
dedicated to the selfless
service of her people.

00:12:00.020 --> 00:12:02.852
We have a great deal to
learn from the community

00:12:02.852 --> 00:12:03.740
that Dr. Abdi has built.

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:06.650
One that continues to
prioritize gender equality

00:12:06.650 --> 00:12:08.750
and solidarity across ethnic lines,

00:12:08.750 --> 00:12:11.033
even in times of unimaginable hardship.

00:12:12.010 --> 00:12:14.750
- And before Dr. Abdi
and Dr. Mohamed speak,

00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:17.670
their work will be introduced
through the following video,

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:21.500
produced in 2013 by the Vital
Voices Global Partnership,

00:12:21.500 --> 00:12:23.690
which works with women
leaders to further women's

00:12:23.690 --> 00:12:26.120
economic empowerment,
political participation,

00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:27.213
and human rights.

00:12:30.710 --> 00:12:32.230
- [Dr. Abdi] My mother was dreaming

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:34.573
that I would be a great person.

00:12:35.810 --> 00:12:38.810
She became pregnant with her seventh child

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:40.637
when I was 11 years old.

00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:45.793
As her stomach swelled,
she felt weakness and pain.

00:12:47.350 --> 00:12:49.700
But she said: no problem, Hawa.

00:12:49.700 --> 00:12:51.133
Everything will be okay.

00:12:52.450 --> 00:12:57.120
Whether it comes to us
through violence, disease,

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:01.113
or even childbirth, death
is not the end of our story.

00:13:02.220 --> 00:13:05.477
You have to get up and
help someone who needs you.

00:13:06.780 --> 00:13:09.730
I became a doctor because
I wanted to save others

00:13:09.730 --> 00:13:12.860
from feeling the pain I
felt when my mother died.

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:18.950
The Somalia I grew up in was
so much different than today.

00:13:18.950 --> 00:13:22.253
Mogadishu was the best
place in all Africa.

00:13:23.811 --> 00:13:26.610
In the rural areas,
children had fresh milk,

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:29.540
fresh meat, fresh air.

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:33.997
Life was very beautiful, very
simple, and very peaceful.

00:13:35.585 --> 00:13:38.910
It started to go bad in 1988,

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:40.493
when the government collapsed.

00:13:41.690 --> 00:13:44.483
More and more people come to
us to escape the fighting.

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:51.230
The Somali people have a
tradition of hospitality.

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:53.490
When a traveler comes to you,

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:57.220
you have to give him the
best of what you have.

00:13:57.220 --> 00:14:00.920
It seems only natural that
the problems we could solve,

00:14:00.920 --> 00:14:03.620
like giving someone a place to stay

00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:06.503
and a feeling of safety, should be solved.

00:14:07.420 --> 00:14:09.300
When space became full,

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:12.730
families began to sleep under the trees.

00:14:12.730 --> 00:14:17.730
After more than 20 years,
it became 90,000 people.

00:14:21.250 --> 00:14:23.023
These people have become my family.

00:14:24.070 --> 00:14:25.990
Here, we are all Somali.

00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:29.913
If you want to identify with
your clan, you cannot stay.

00:14:31.570 --> 00:14:34.110
In time, my children become doctors,

00:14:34.110 --> 00:14:36.313
and now we work together.

00:14:37.590 --> 00:14:40.290
Each crisis we face gives way to the next,

00:14:40.290 --> 00:14:44.140
as a new group takes control of our area.

00:14:44.140 --> 00:14:46.940
Sometimes, these children with their guns,

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:49.520
they told me: you are an old woman.

00:14:49.520 --> 00:14:51.390
You cannot be in charge.

00:14:51.390 --> 00:14:54.390
I said: and you are a strong young man.

00:14:54.390 --> 00:14:57.440
What have you done for these people?

00:14:57.440 --> 00:14:59.620
Whether through fighting or famine,

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:02.817
we have lost as many as 50 people per day.

00:15:05.250 --> 00:15:07.993
But we get up, we go on.

00:15:11.587 --> 00:15:13.573
And we help someone who needs us.

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:18.253
Today, our one-room clinic
is a 400-bed hospital.

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:23.030
I live for the hope that peace will come.

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:25.300
That my children and my grandchildren

00:15:25.300 --> 00:15:27.943
will someday know the Somalia I loved.

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:32.853
Until that time, they can
build on what I have started.

00:15:33.940 --> 00:15:35.960
I am Dr. Hawa Abdi.

00:15:35.960 --> 00:15:37.303
This is my vital voice.

00:15:38.762 --> 00:15:40.813
Now, raise yours.

00:15:40.813 --> 00:15:43.396
(somber music)

00:15:50.163 --> 00:15:52.580
(applauding)

00:16:00.090 --> 00:16:03.076
- Please join us in
welcoming Dr. Hawa Abdi

00:16:03.076 --> 00:16:04.985
and Dr. Deqo Mohamed, once again.

00:16:04.985 --> 00:16:07.402
(applauding)

00:16:13.540 --> 00:16:16.290
- So, thank you so much for coming here.

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:18.410
As I said, this is a true honor

00:16:18.410 --> 00:16:20.240
to have you here on Yale's campus,

00:16:20.240 --> 00:16:22.460
and to talk to this wonderful audience,

00:16:22.460 --> 00:16:24.440
who's here to learn about your work.

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:29.210
With Hawa Abdi Village and
your long career of service

00:16:29.210 --> 00:16:30.990
to Somalia.

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:34.170
I just wanted to start
by asking you a question

00:16:34.170 --> 00:16:37.320
about what you remember,
growing up in the Somalia

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:39.800
that had undergone so many changes,

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:44.230
from independence onward, and
how you came to the decision

00:16:44.230 --> 00:16:46.933
to become a doctor, to serve your people?

00:16:49.467 --> 00:16:51.800
(murmuring)

00:16:52.770 --> 00:16:56.020
- When I was a child and died my mother,

00:16:56.020 --> 00:17:00.790
I felt bad, and I decided at
that time to become a doctor.

00:17:00.790 --> 00:17:02.343
To save mothers' lives.

00:17:05.177 --> 00:17:09.140
My father was kind and he was intelligent.

00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:12.440
All his friends come at that time.

00:17:12.440 --> 00:17:16.380
To send a girl outside was very difficult.

00:17:16.380 --> 00:17:18.763
For Somali, the traditional way.

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:22.621
But he send me, because he could,

00:17:22.621 --> 00:17:24.290
and he was understanding that studying

00:17:24.290 --> 00:17:27.060
is the very best things of life.

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:29.433
He sent me to Soviet Union,

00:17:31.520 --> 00:17:34.163
and when my dream became,

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:42.240
when my dream became over,

00:17:44.090 --> 00:17:46.140
I decided to study well.

00:17:46.140 --> 00:17:48.500
When I went into the Soviet Union,

00:17:48.500 --> 00:17:53.500
I was studying at night and
day and became a doctor.

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:55.603
To save other mothers.

00:17:58.730 --> 00:18:02.713
So, I finished my study after seven years.

00:18:05.833 --> 00:18:08.630
I come back, I began
to work the hospitals,

00:18:08.630 --> 00:18:10.840
because with troubles in Mogadishu,

00:18:10.840 --> 00:18:14.053
I have seen that the
justice is not very good.

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:18.673
Then I began to study the faculty of law.

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:25.460
In 1972, I finished in
1971, medical school.

00:18:28.750 --> 00:18:31.130
Then I finished also the law school.

00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:37.050
I was working in big hospitals,

00:18:37.050 --> 00:18:41.540
but always they would brought
in us a mother who was

00:18:43.089 --> 00:18:47.333
already nearly to die, because
she lost a lot of blood.

00:18:49.250 --> 00:18:54.250
Or the child is not family, the
delivery was very difficult.

00:18:55.580 --> 00:18:59.829
In rural areas, they were
bringing us many mothers

00:18:59.829 --> 00:19:01.929
who were dying before we had done nothing.

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:06.910
So I decided to go to the rural area

00:19:06.910 --> 00:19:10.063
to help women in rural areas.

00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:13.430
- So how long were you
working in Mogadishu,

00:19:13.430 --> 00:19:15.820
before you decided to open the clinic?

00:19:15.820 --> 00:19:20.820
- I finish in '71, and I went
out to the rural area in 1983.

00:19:25.070 --> 00:19:29.110
- And did you continue your
relationship to the hospitals

00:19:29.110 --> 00:19:33.270
in Mogadishu, or did you
completely just build a brand new--

00:19:33.270 --> 00:19:38.270
- No, I was having Mogadishu's
hospitals with my friend,

00:19:40.410 --> 00:19:44.380
who was there working, and
we were, they were coming

00:19:45.878 --> 00:19:50.500
Sundays, we have Fridays, and
on Fridays they were coming

00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:54.890
to me, because the city sometimes,

00:19:54.890 --> 00:19:59.890
they want to go outside,
breathe the fresh air.

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:06.580
We were having a relation
with the big hospitals

00:20:06.580 --> 00:20:07.593
in Mogadishu,

00:20:10.420 --> 00:20:14.323
but at 1990, it broke out civil war.

00:20:15.950 --> 00:20:18.620
This civil war was
caused because in Africa,

00:20:18.620 --> 00:20:22.443
all we have this which
is called: tribalism.

00:20:23.330 --> 00:20:28.330
Tribalism, two clans
were becoming to fight.

00:20:29.450 --> 00:20:33.760
One clan would be stronger
than the other, one weak.

00:20:33.760 --> 00:20:36.983
They will kill and
destroy all the property.

00:20:38.770 --> 00:20:41.460
- Can you say a little bit
about what your clinic was like

00:20:41.460 --> 00:20:44.310
before the civil war broke out in 1991?

00:20:44.310 --> 00:20:47.092
- I began a one-room clinic.

00:20:47.092 --> 00:20:48.190
- A one-room clinic.

00:20:48.190 --> 00:20:51.240
- But those, the rural areas,
sometimes they don't want

00:20:51.240 --> 00:20:52.977
to go to the hospitals.

00:20:54.140 --> 00:20:57.700
They prefer their traditional medicine.

00:20:57.700 --> 00:21:01.400
So to convince them was very difficult.

00:21:01.400 --> 00:21:06.400
But at last they understood
that the hospital can help them.

00:21:10.679 --> 00:21:12.200
So they come to me.

00:21:12.200 --> 00:21:15.173
Many patients, maybe hundreds about.

00:21:16.790 --> 00:21:20.560
Numbers, my numbers was hundred per day.

00:21:20.560 --> 00:21:22.495
- At the peak was 100 per day?

00:21:22.495 --> 00:21:23.940
- Yes, yes, yes.

00:21:23.940 --> 00:21:26.897
- But at first, did you
have difficulty getting, ah,

00:21:28.580 --> 00:21:30.970
getting local people
to come to the clinic?

00:21:30.970 --> 00:21:33.160
Is that what you were saying?

00:21:33.160 --> 00:21:36.028
- 10 or 20 before.

00:21:36.028 --> 00:21:37.769
In the first few days.

00:21:37.769 --> 00:21:39.170
- The first few days, yes.

00:21:39.170 --> 00:21:43.210
- Then we began to, with my daughter Deqo,

00:21:43.210 --> 00:21:48.210
going to the small, small
villages, every Friday.

00:21:48.560 --> 00:21:52.490
And to us, if there is a child sick,

00:21:52.490 --> 00:21:57.103
or a woman sick, or a, we
have plenty of malaria.

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:00.083
We were bringing our medicine.

00:22:01.320 --> 00:22:05.790
I was checking when I
would go, in a white coat,

00:22:05.790 --> 00:22:08.713
the children first, then
they were running from us.

00:22:11.350 --> 00:22:16.200
Then, they adopted us because the child,

00:22:16.200 --> 00:22:21.200
when the child is sick, Deqo
was distributing medicine.

00:22:23.210 --> 00:22:28.210
I visited, we have our medicine,
I write a prescription,

00:22:29.719 --> 00:22:34.640
Deqo will bring and will
distribute, or to give the child,

00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:36.423
which is sick, or the women.

00:22:37.510 --> 00:22:39.580
- And how old was Deqo at the time?

00:22:39.580 --> 00:22:42.253
When she was traveling with
you in the rural areas?

00:22:44.600 --> 00:22:47.150
- I was 10 or 12, I think, so very young.

00:22:47.150 --> 00:22:51.093
- Yes, yes, she was very, very small.

00:22:52.600 --> 00:22:55.310
- I think what she meant,
because of Somali culture

00:22:55.310 --> 00:22:58.330
and tradition, hospital
is seen as a death place.

00:22:58.330 --> 00:23:00.520
So, because they are
arriving, the patient comes

00:23:00.520 --> 00:23:03.350
to the hospital, last minute, and were,

00:23:03.350 --> 00:23:05.400
we don't have a well-equipped hospital,

00:23:05.400 --> 00:23:07.420
so we're losing a lot of patients.

00:23:07.420 --> 00:23:10.570
So they see it's a disaster place to go.

00:23:10.570 --> 00:23:13.960
So when in the beginning
they didn't come to her,

00:23:13.960 --> 00:23:17.220
she came up with this mobile clinic,

00:23:17.220 --> 00:23:20.180
where we go out and go
to them in the villages.

00:23:20.180 --> 00:23:23.800
So it was very fun, just
to be around and traveling.

00:23:23.800 --> 00:23:26.070
And as a kid, we were just having fun.

00:23:26.070 --> 00:23:29.440
So I learned all the medicine,
antibiotics, parasetamol,

00:23:29.440 --> 00:23:32.421
the various names, and
they were just like,

00:23:32.421 --> 00:23:34.180
give 10, 15, so we were being heard from.

00:23:34.180 --> 00:23:38.963
I was a pharmacist earlier
than I become a doctor.

00:23:38.963 --> 00:23:41.200
(laughing)

00:23:41.200 --> 00:23:43.760
- Did you incorporate
traditional methods of healing,

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:45.323
as part of your practice?

00:23:46.442 --> 00:23:49.200
(speaking foreign language)

00:23:49.200 --> 00:23:50.440
- Sometimes.

00:23:50.440 --> 00:23:52.380
Sometimes I was doing.

00:23:52.380 --> 00:23:56.422
Sometimes more effective,
traditional medicine,

00:23:56.422 --> 00:23:58.043
than other modern medicine.

00:23:59.880 --> 00:24:00.930
Especially in Africa.

00:24:02.850 --> 00:24:05.360
- So I imagine this must have
been a very peaceful time

00:24:05.360 --> 00:24:07.967
of practicing before the war.

00:24:07.967 --> 00:24:10.033
- Yes, yes, very peaceful time.

00:24:11.330 --> 00:24:13.150
- And so can you describe how it changed

00:24:13.150 --> 00:24:15.460
after the outbreak of the civil war?

00:24:15.460 --> 00:24:17.554
How did that affect your clinic.

00:24:17.554 --> 00:24:18.773
- Oh, it was a disaster.

00:24:18.773 --> 00:24:21.683
There was no one would go to his job.

00:24:22.840 --> 00:24:27.840
Africa, we live with what we
have: the farms and animals.

00:24:30.880 --> 00:24:34.640
Especially women of
Africa are very strong.

00:24:34.640 --> 00:24:37.290
They were carrying their
family with their hands,

00:24:37.290 --> 00:24:40.783
because they are digging
farmers, leading animals,

00:24:41.836 --> 00:24:44.386
and they are carrying their
children on their back.

00:24:46.890 --> 00:24:51.890
So, we had a difficult time
after broke out civil war.

00:24:56.590 --> 00:24:59.723
Because no one can go and do his job.

00:25:01.290 --> 00:25:03.150
- Do you still remember the first families

00:25:03.150 --> 00:25:05.680
that came to your clinic for assistance?

00:25:05.680 --> 00:25:08.916
- People coming, continuing to come to me.

00:25:08.916 --> 00:25:09.749
All my friends.

00:25:10.883 --> 00:25:12.750
(speaking foreign language)

00:25:12.750 --> 00:25:17.750
It was 1,500 families, first.

00:25:17.983 --> 00:25:20.470
- 1,500 families?

00:25:20.470 --> 00:25:22.950
In the first week, or?

00:25:22.950 --> 00:25:24.768
- In the first months.

00:25:24.768 --> 00:25:25.890
- First months, I see.

00:25:25.890 --> 00:25:29.440
And then I know from
reading the autobiography

00:25:29.440 --> 00:25:31.300
that they just kept coming.

00:25:31.300 --> 00:25:35.620
- Yeah, they were coming
then after, bit by bit,

00:25:35.620 --> 00:25:38.063
until they reached 15,000 families.

00:25:39.350 --> 00:25:42.898
- At what point was it that
it reached 15,000 families?

00:25:42.898 --> 00:25:44.090
After how many months?

00:25:44.090 --> 00:25:45.423
- Two years.
- Two years?

00:25:46.340 --> 00:25:49.763
- Years, we have to peak in 2008,

00:25:49.763 --> 00:25:54.380
when the war is exacerbated
and all three million

00:25:54.380 --> 00:25:57.280
of Mogadishu residents just had to flee

00:25:57.280 --> 00:26:00.880
in a couple of days, because
of a severe clash happened

00:26:00.880 --> 00:26:03.463
between the government and Al-Shabaab,

00:26:04.341 --> 00:26:06.230
and Ethiopian was invading at that time.

00:26:06.230 --> 00:26:08.890
It was Islamic corps, so
it was a political mess.

00:26:08.890 --> 00:26:12.050
So the city became a battle zone,

00:26:12.050 --> 00:26:14.400
so three million people had to flee.

00:26:14.400 --> 00:26:16.540
And in our hospital,
it's right on the road

00:26:16.540 --> 00:26:19.510
where you leave the city;
we're only 20 kilometers away

00:26:19.510 --> 00:26:23.244
from Mogadishu, so that's where we were.

00:26:23.244 --> 00:26:24.377
We just took as much as we could,

00:26:24.377 --> 00:26:26.410
and when it reached up to 90,000,

00:26:26.410 --> 00:26:30.582
we didn't have any space to
have them, so we just had to--

00:26:30.582 --> 00:26:32.200
- And it's an impossibility to feed them.

00:26:32.200 --> 00:26:34.320
Or to give water.

00:26:34.320 --> 00:26:38.600
So I began to appeal to
international organizations,

00:26:38.600 --> 00:26:43.600
like ISTRC, like WFP, UNICEF and AMICEF.

00:26:46.620 --> 00:26:51.021
They come to me
immediately, giving me food.

00:26:51.021 --> 00:26:54.680
(unintelligible muttering)

00:26:54.680 --> 00:26:57.113
And they're giving me fuel,

00:26:59.170 --> 00:27:04.170
because we had a well, but to
bring water, it takes fuel.

00:27:05.250 --> 00:27:08.293
They were giving me fuel, food, shelter.

00:27:09.220 --> 00:27:13.480
They were giving borscht, medicine,

00:27:13.480 --> 00:27:15.503
they're giving us medicine.

00:27:19.740 --> 00:27:22.260
- I imagine when it first began,

00:27:22.260 --> 00:27:25.440
you had no idea how
long it would go on for.

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:27.760
And of course, it went on for decades.

00:27:27.760 --> 00:27:30.410
I'm curious, at what point did you realize

00:27:30.410 --> 00:27:33.520
this wasn't just going to
be a temporary shelter,

00:27:33.520 --> 00:27:35.450
or relief center?

00:27:35.450 --> 00:27:37.830
- There were international organizations,

00:27:37.830 --> 00:27:40.743
they were giving us plastic shelters.

00:27:42.158 --> 00:27:45.825
(speaking foreign language)

00:27:47.700 --> 00:27:51.120
I was always thinking
that maybe one week after

00:27:51.120 --> 00:27:56.120
would come the peace, but
peace went far, far, far.

00:27:56.143 --> 00:27:58.493
Until now, for 27 years.

00:28:00.470 --> 00:28:04.740
- Yeah, so what, can you describe what,

00:28:04.740 --> 00:28:07.170
clearly you went through
many, many hardships

00:28:07.170 --> 00:28:10.110
in dealing with the magnitude.

00:28:10.110 --> 00:28:15.110
- It was bad, it was
seeing very bad things,

00:28:17.280 --> 00:28:22.280
you know, without, with
bothering poor people,

00:28:23.220 --> 00:28:27.520
and treating bad, but
you cannot do nothing.

00:28:27.520 --> 00:28:29.653
You are only one person, a woman.

00:28:30.720 --> 00:28:31.563
In Africa.

00:28:34.196 --> 00:28:35.296
It was very difficult.

00:28:36.340 --> 00:28:38.647
I have witness of many bad things.

00:28:41.520 --> 00:28:46.520
- You created a state within
the state for that 25 years.

00:28:48.667 --> 00:28:51.610
- These people are
fighting because of clan.

00:28:53.070 --> 00:28:58.070
In Africa, we have tribalism,
which is worse than terrorism.

00:28:59.120 --> 00:29:01.100
- Tribalism is worse than terrorism?

00:29:01.100 --> 00:29:05.110
- Tribalism is way worse than terrorists.

00:29:05.110 --> 00:29:08.870
Because terrorism, if you
get the law and power,

00:29:08.870 --> 00:29:10.730
you can eradicate it.

00:29:10.730 --> 00:29:14.763
But tribalism, two clans will fight,

00:29:16.000 --> 00:29:20.400
some will be beaten, but
those clan was beaten,

00:29:20.400 --> 00:29:21.897
they will tell all their children,

00:29:21.897 --> 00:29:24.890
and those children will
tell other, other children.

00:29:24.890 --> 00:29:26.820
So it's never finished.

00:29:26.820 --> 00:29:29.950
- Generation to generation.
- Generations.

00:29:29.950 --> 00:29:33.030
- So that's why you studied
the law, I think so.

00:29:33.030 --> 00:29:38.030
- Then, family, strong
family and weak families,

00:29:38.318 --> 00:29:41.330
they will fight, the
strong family will come

00:29:41.330 --> 00:29:45.253
and beat those weak families,
which is my village.

00:29:47.144 --> 00:29:50.400
So, I thought: how to
live with these people?

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:51.473
How to command?

00:29:52.830 --> 00:29:56.310
I said, ah, but we with internal rule,

00:29:56.310 --> 00:30:00.230
I don't know, no clan division in Somalia.

00:30:00.230 --> 00:30:04.153
If you come to my village,
you are Somali only.

00:30:05.060 --> 00:30:06.890
If you are strong, it's up to you.

00:30:06.890 --> 00:30:08.983
You go, you are a clan.

00:30:10.340 --> 00:30:14.480
If you come to me, I will
give you space to live,

00:30:14.480 --> 00:30:18.383
clean water, healthy water, free.

00:30:20.140 --> 00:30:25.140
If you are begin to say
that: I'm that clan,

00:30:25.620 --> 00:30:28.070
I will beat this weak clan.

00:30:28.070 --> 00:30:28.907
You will go out.

00:30:30.470 --> 00:30:34.233
So, the people in my
village became equal then.

00:30:35.370 --> 00:30:38.730
They did not fight another time.

00:30:38.730 --> 00:30:43.730
There was no powerful clan who
was pretending to be higher

00:30:44.380 --> 00:30:45.433
over other families.

00:30:46.530 --> 00:30:47.573
They became equal.

00:30:48.410 --> 00:30:51.040
- So you were able to
create the equal society,

00:30:51.040 --> 00:30:56.040
not allowing for clan divisions,
but instead being Somali?

00:30:56.122 --> 00:30:57.260
- Yes, yes.

00:30:57.260 --> 00:31:00.050
- So I'm sure that was
not an easy process.

00:31:00.050 --> 00:31:05.050
Were there people who
still refused not to have

00:31:05.250 --> 00:31:07.330
their clan allegiances, but, yeah,

00:31:07.330 --> 00:31:08.743
how did you deal with that?

00:31:09.610 --> 00:31:11.853
- Then, after a few months,

00:31:13.550 --> 00:31:18.393
it begins a family violence.

00:31:20.190 --> 00:31:23.680
The men becoming to beat their wives,

00:31:23.680 --> 00:31:26.940
because men was working the day,

00:31:26.940 --> 00:31:28.340
and coming when (muttering),

00:31:30.930 --> 00:31:33.960
and he saw all his friends.

00:31:33.960 --> 00:31:38.260
Now they are standing in
under the plastic shed,

00:31:38.260 --> 00:31:41.270
plastic, plastic--

00:31:41.270 --> 00:31:42.722
- Sheets.

00:31:42.722 --> 00:31:44.120
- Sheet.

00:31:44.120 --> 00:31:46.713
My father, mother and children.

00:31:48.691 --> 00:31:52.750
And when they took, he
began to beat his wife.

00:31:54.270 --> 00:31:59.270
But family violence came up, up, up.

00:31:59.760 --> 00:32:04.760
Children are running, because
they love their mother

00:32:05.027 --> 00:32:09.260
and their father, but
father is beating mother.

00:32:09.260 --> 00:32:12.080
They are running to me: Mama Hawa,

00:32:12.080 --> 00:32:14.003
our father is beating our mother.

00:32:15.285 --> 00:32:17.273
And I've sent some people.

00:32:19.010 --> 00:32:24.010
We had others, it lead
us to do some decision.

00:32:25.380 --> 00:32:30.380
You cannot beat, but you
have, I said: bring him.

00:32:30.750 --> 00:32:32.640
And with the jail.

00:32:32.640 --> 00:32:33.660
- And put him in jail?

00:32:33.660 --> 00:32:35.180
- Yes, and they did.

00:32:35.180 --> 00:32:36.350
We are (unintelligible).

00:32:37.490 --> 00:32:40.480
We would open some, these youth people,

00:32:40.480 --> 00:32:43.403
would bring him and put in the jail.

00:32:44.690 --> 00:32:46.810
- And you should describe
what this jail was,

00:32:46.810 --> 00:32:49.887
because I'm not sure everyone knows.

00:32:49.887 --> 00:32:51.652
(laughing)

00:32:51.652 --> 00:32:53.542
(muttering)

00:32:53.542 --> 00:32:54.667
- It was only store.

00:32:54.667 --> 00:32:55.665
- Storage?

00:32:55.665 --> 00:32:56.661
- Storage, yes.

00:32:56.661 --> 00:32:57.688
- A storage closet.

00:32:57.688 --> 00:32:59.401
- Yes, a storage closet.

00:32:59.401 --> 00:33:02.027
They would sit down there,
and after a few days,

00:33:03.430 --> 00:33:07.357
we are not talking with
them, until the elders

00:33:07.357 --> 00:33:10.750
and women, or the youth committee,

00:33:10.750 --> 00:33:14.083
will decide this case, he will stay there.

00:33:17.660 --> 00:33:21.378
- So you basically created rule of law.

00:33:21.378 --> 00:33:23.313
Or the, ah--

00:33:23.313 --> 00:33:24.910
- But there's just two rules.

00:33:24.910 --> 00:33:26.653
Two simple rules to stay with me.

00:33:27.740 --> 00:33:29.430
First, it's Somali in my village.

00:33:29.430 --> 00:33:33.480
They become equal, without any pretending

00:33:33.480 --> 00:33:35.763
that he come from a strong clan.

00:33:37.180 --> 00:33:40.230
- So everyone's equal, no
one's pretending to be,

00:33:40.230 --> 00:33:42.790
or claiming to be from a
stronger or better clan?

00:33:42.790 --> 00:33:43.623
- Yeah, yes.

00:33:44.583 --> 00:33:45.839
- And the second rule?

00:33:45.839 --> 00:33:50.663
- The second rule, the
men stop beating his wife.

00:33:51.527 --> 00:33:53.335
- There would be no
domestic violence, yeah.

00:33:53.335 --> 00:33:55.752
(applauding)

00:33:59.164 --> 00:34:00.720
- And it's, I've heard
it's very effective,

00:34:00.720 --> 00:34:02.616
being in the storage closet.

00:34:02.616 --> 00:34:04.170
(laughing)

00:34:04.170 --> 00:34:06.940
- It is effective, if
you're in a dark small room

00:34:06.940 --> 00:34:09.290
with a bunch of food sacks around you,

00:34:09.290 --> 00:34:13.410
and you get claustrophobia
in that little thing.

00:34:13.410 --> 00:34:17.260
But it works, most of it,
the majority of the time.

00:34:17.260 --> 00:34:20.420
But it took, I think,
with my mom's persistence,

00:34:20.420 --> 00:34:22.470
and 25 years of living there.

00:34:22.470 --> 00:34:25.050
So in the beginning, the
people left, some of them,

00:34:25.050 --> 00:34:27.810
it was some clashes, but finally,

00:34:27.810 --> 00:34:30.930
by the time I arrived in 2007,

00:34:30.930 --> 00:34:34.690
the rule was in place,
so they were teaching me.

00:34:34.690 --> 00:34:37.070
Some of them forgot I was
there in the beginning,

00:34:37.070 --> 00:34:38.600
when the rule was implemented.

00:34:38.600 --> 00:34:41.630
It's like: make sure if there
is any domestic violence,

00:34:41.630 --> 00:34:43.550
you immediately arrest the husband.

00:34:43.550 --> 00:34:46.000
And it's like: okay, I will do it.

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:47.917
(laughing)

00:34:47.917 --> 00:34:50.610
So the committee was telling
me one day when I was there:

00:34:50.610 --> 00:34:52.120
that's the rule, your mom did it.

00:34:52.120 --> 00:34:55.683
So my mom basically
acts as a supreme court.

00:34:55.683 --> 00:34:57.118
- She's the supreme court?

00:34:57.118 --> 00:34:58.000
(laughing)

00:34:58.000 --> 00:35:01.830
- The committee decides how
things should go, the youth,

00:35:01.830 --> 00:35:04.680
the young generation
who grew up in the camp,

00:35:04.680 --> 00:35:06.560
are doing the police action, you know,

00:35:06.560 --> 00:35:10.320
arresting and all that,
but my mom is the final,

00:35:10.320 --> 00:35:13.390
if they cannot decide,
she's the supreme court,

00:35:13.390 --> 00:35:16.090
where we should appeal if we
could not decide the cases.

00:35:16.090 --> 00:35:20.990
So finally, my, all my friends and I,

00:35:20.990 --> 00:35:23.207
we used to have our fun and say:

00:35:23.207 --> 00:35:24.650
mom, you never get to practice your law.

00:35:24.650 --> 00:35:27.304
So this is the opportunity. (laughing)

00:35:27.304 --> 00:35:29.154
You practice your law, after the war.

00:35:31.160 --> 00:35:32.750
- It's held with me.

00:35:32.750 --> 00:35:35.723
And also, it's helped with
the society of my village.

00:35:37.930 --> 00:35:38.763
- That's great.

00:35:39.659 --> 00:35:41.770
What about maintaining structure and order

00:35:41.770 --> 00:35:45.130
in terms of food distribution
and resources and water,

00:35:45.130 --> 00:35:47.360
when all of that is in such short supply?

00:35:47.360 --> 00:35:50.280
It must be very challenging to be as fair

00:35:50.280 --> 00:35:54.150
as you possibly can with
all the needy families.

00:35:54.150 --> 00:35:58.340
- We decided we'd divide
in the village, six parts.

00:35:58.340 --> 00:36:01.200
Every part they were
having their committee

00:36:02.820 --> 00:36:06.803
who will bring his part his food, water,

00:36:07.820 --> 00:36:11.653
and also cleaning the village.

00:36:13.010 --> 00:36:15.033
Sanitation of the village.

00:36:16.475 --> 00:36:18.687
They are responsible, this committee.

00:36:21.010 --> 00:36:24.860
- And I think the majority
would make more beautiful

00:36:24.860 --> 00:36:28.470
the structure, and the
system is people find a life.

00:36:28.470 --> 00:36:31.150
Because the majority of the camp is IDP's,

00:36:31.150 --> 00:36:33.210
internally displaced
people, and in Somalia,

00:36:33.210 --> 00:36:35.530
they're just sitting and awaiting aid,

00:36:35.530 --> 00:36:37.600
or there's a clash that's happening.

00:36:37.600 --> 00:36:41.010
So within our village, they have a life.

00:36:41.010 --> 00:36:43.740
They wake up in the
morning, the elderly gather,

00:36:43.740 --> 00:36:45.870
and they decide what is the problem next,

00:36:45.870 --> 00:36:47.530
how we could solve.

00:36:47.530 --> 00:36:49.930
The young generation cleans up, you know,

00:36:49.930 --> 00:36:52.980
acts as the police, so you could feel,

00:36:52.980 --> 00:36:54.800
you know, become more alive.

00:36:54.800 --> 00:36:57.120
So you don't have to keep telling them,

00:36:57.120 --> 00:36:59.240
it's part of their daily routine,

00:36:59.240 --> 00:37:00.830
and they take very proudly.

00:37:00.830 --> 00:37:03.370
They say: oh, we are Hawa Abdi Village,

00:37:03.370 --> 00:37:04.990
we have this, this is our rules.

00:37:04.990 --> 00:37:06.780
They tell everyone who comes to it.

00:37:06.780 --> 00:37:09.750
So it's, it was fascinating me being away,

00:37:09.750 --> 00:37:11.640
after 10 years and coming back,

00:37:11.640 --> 00:37:13.243
and seeing the transformation.

00:37:16.500 --> 00:37:19.110
- So, one of the most well-known incidents

00:37:19.110 --> 00:37:23.580
that made international
headlines was in 2010,

00:37:23.580 --> 00:37:27.690
when Hizbul Islam basically
tried to take over the camp,

00:37:27.690 --> 00:37:31.190
and my understanding is
you were put under arrest.

00:37:31.190 --> 00:37:34.270
They were trying to force you
to relinquish full control.

00:37:34.270 --> 00:37:36.610
That was mentioned in the video, yes.

00:37:36.610 --> 00:37:39.713
- 2010, it made.

00:37:43.209 --> 00:37:46.780
The come, a group of
Islamist, Hizbul Islam,

00:37:49.030 --> 00:37:54.030
they come and they say:
you are woman and old.

00:37:54.300 --> 00:37:56.593
You cannot run this
camp with these people.

00:37:58.230 --> 00:38:03.230
You are over, you have
to hand over and give us.

00:38:04.757 --> 00:38:07.263
We will command; we are
young and we are men.

00:38:08.840 --> 00:38:12.900
Okay, I say, you are men
because you have two testes.

00:38:12.900 --> 00:38:15.977
But the goats also-- (laughing)

00:38:17.640 --> 00:38:19.618
- It's okay, you're both doctors.

00:38:19.618 --> 00:38:21.314
(laughing)

00:38:21.314 --> 00:38:23.860
- You have two testes, but
the goat also has two testes.

00:38:23.860 --> 00:38:26.740
What you have done to serve your society?

00:38:26.740 --> 00:38:29.360
The people was with me, they come,

00:38:29.360 --> 00:38:32.810
say: you keep quiet, they may kill you.

00:38:32.810 --> 00:38:35.990
No, I say, I will tell what's the truth.

00:38:35.990 --> 00:38:38.650
What they have done for their society,

00:38:38.650 --> 00:38:43.650
but they are coming to tell
me to hand over this camp,

00:38:43.970 --> 00:38:45.203
or this village?

00:38:46.610 --> 00:38:48.150
You are a woman and old.

00:38:48.150 --> 00:38:50.610
You cannot run this job.

00:38:50.610 --> 00:38:55.610
You are not in a position
to run this village.

00:38:57.150 --> 00:39:00.621
I said: I am sorry, but
you have not done nothing

00:39:00.621 --> 00:39:01.471
for this society.

00:39:03.620 --> 00:39:06.673
If you are men, it's up to you.

00:39:09.840 --> 00:39:12.660
- Were you not scared at
all, during this whole time?

00:39:12.660 --> 00:39:15.080
Because they had weapons, if I understand,

00:39:15.080 --> 00:39:16.941
very scary weapons.

00:39:16.941 --> 00:39:20.010
- Yes, yes. (laughing)

00:39:20.010 --> 00:39:25.010
One of my friends, he says
like: Hawa, keep quiet!

00:39:26.350 --> 00:39:27.873
Not say nothing like that.

00:39:30.508 --> 00:39:33.677
(speaking foreign language)

00:39:33.677 --> 00:39:37.200
- Yeah, so, but they come and attacked.

00:39:37.200 --> 00:39:41.120
- And after a few days,
they surrounded us,

00:39:41.120 --> 00:39:44.498
and they send all the
weapons with heavy shelling

00:39:44.498 --> 00:39:49.340
in the hospital, in my camp, in my house.

00:39:49.340 --> 00:39:54.340
In the pediatrician
ward and maternity ward,

00:39:54.790 --> 00:39:57.910
they send us, and at that
time, there was water

00:39:59.615 --> 00:40:03.240
in the area, and many
children were admitted

00:40:03.240 --> 00:40:08.240
in that pediatric ward with
fluid, water in the brain.

00:40:12.141 --> 00:40:13.530
- The hospital was working fully.

00:40:13.530 --> 00:40:18.530
- Yes, and there was a
woman, where we operated,

00:40:20.800 --> 00:40:24.563
I operated, C-section, took the child,

00:40:27.110 --> 00:40:30.780
they're lying on their beds,

00:40:30.780 --> 00:40:33.357
but after heavy shelling, they run.

00:40:35.107 --> 00:40:40.107
And some, one of those
women opened up four stitch.

00:40:44.689 --> 00:40:46.350
- But luckily, the patient didn't die.

00:40:46.350 --> 00:40:49.427
One of our security guards passed away,

00:40:49.427 --> 00:40:51.983
and was killed during the attack.

00:40:53.146 --> 00:40:58.146
- And they come and
say, during the attack,

00:40:58.410 --> 00:41:01.743
my daughter is connecting with BBC Somali,

00:41:03.680 --> 00:41:05.843
Somali language broadcast.

00:41:07.847 --> 00:41:12.340
And I tell that Hizbul Islam attacked me,

00:41:12.340 --> 00:41:15.527
and my people now, they are,

00:41:16.610 --> 00:41:19.790
they want to destroy our
hospital and my people.

00:41:19.790 --> 00:41:22.590
I don't know how many will remain.

00:41:22.590 --> 00:41:26.837
I made here, (vocalizing), a big noise.

00:41:29.540 --> 00:41:33.150
And all the Somali here, they come,

00:41:34.840 --> 00:41:39.840
they come after and
they: you talk outside.

00:41:39.860 --> 00:41:41.633
Give your mobile.

00:41:43.130 --> 00:41:46.480
With me were five of my nurses.

00:41:46.480 --> 00:41:48.220
They took all of our mobiles.

00:41:50.690 --> 00:41:55.493
They brought the minibus,
and they say: stand up.

00:41:57.060 --> 00:41:58.173
Me, first.

00:41:59.280 --> 00:42:00.613
Okay, I stood up.

00:42:01.530 --> 00:42:05.560
The other children, the
other nurse also stand up.

00:42:05.560 --> 00:42:06.393
Come.

00:42:07.860 --> 00:42:12.040
I went after them, we all went after them.

00:42:12.040 --> 00:42:14.763
They put us in a minibus.

00:42:18.140 --> 00:42:20.360
All the people were crying.

00:42:20.360 --> 00:42:22.253
They thought that they would kill us,

00:42:23.172 --> 00:42:24.270
but they did not kill us.

00:42:24.270 --> 00:42:29.270
They brought us somewhere
where they have their offices.

00:42:31.160 --> 00:42:32.410
And they closed one room.

00:42:33.490 --> 00:42:35.630
- They basically arrested you

00:42:35.630 --> 00:42:37.860
and have you there in 10 hours.

00:42:37.860 --> 00:42:40.500
- Yes, in 10 hours we were there.

00:42:40.500 --> 00:42:42.030
- Under house arrest?

00:42:42.030 --> 00:42:43.800
- Yeah, no.

00:42:43.800 --> 00:42:46.740
- Yeah, before the house
arrest, they took her

00:42:46.740 --> 00:42:51.740
to their place, and after
she live-broadcasted.

00:42:52.109 --> 00:42:55.310
(overlapping interjections)

00:42:55.310 --> 00:42:57.076
- They bring me my phone.

00:42:57.076 --> 00:42:59.490
They say: you have many supporters.

00:42:59.490 --> 00:43:01.820
Tell your people that we don't harmed you,

00:43:01.820 --> 00:43:05.473
that you are safe, you are healthy.

00:43:06.470 --> 00:43:09.533
I talked first to my daughters.

00:43:11.669 --> 00:43:13.970
And I say: I'm alive, I'm okay.

00:43:13.970 --> 00:43:15.013
I have no problem.

00:43:17.090 --> 00:43:20.040
- And it was the longest 10
hours of my life, I think.

00:43:20.040 --> 00:43:23.130
So, we didn't know where she is,

00:43:23.130 --> 00:43:25.340
for her persistence in being strong,

00:43:25.340 --> 00:43:29.913
but still, we were all blaming
her in somehow, you know?

00:43:30.980 --> 00:43:33.330
She shouldn't say: resist them.

00:43:33.330 --> 00:43:35.483
But we're lucky to have her back.

00:43:36.670 --> 00:43:40.750
The live broadcast, I think,
made them more aggressive

00:43:40.750 --> 00:43:45.550
and made them more mad,
because the all-over Somalia

00:43:45.550 --> 00:43:48.973
was listening to their
shelling, on the radio.

00:43:49.916 --> 00:43:51.040
So the whole country was shocked.

00:43:51.040 --> 00:43:53.650
Like, how in the world
you can attack a hospital?

00:43:53.650 --> 00:43:55.520
Especially Mama Hawa?

00:43:55.520 --> 00:43:59.710
So everyone was calling
them and then they get

00:44:00.620 --> 00:44:02.530
very scared and they give her the phone

00:44:02.530 --> 00:44:05.177
and they say: you need
to talk to your people.

00:44:05.177 --> 00:44:06.380
You're still alive, we didn't kill you.

00:44:06.380 --> 00:44:09.710
Just tell them so they get off
of our shoulders, you know?

00:44:09.710 --> 00:44:12.610
They're pushing us,
making sure you're alive.

00:44:12.610 --> 00:44:15.290
And she talked to them, she talked to us,

00:44:15.290 --> 00:44:18.334
and we told all our community--

00:44:18.334 --> 00:44:19.250
- All our friends, also.

00:44:19.250 --> 00:44:21.890
- You're alive, and then
they told her: we destroy

00:44:21.890 --> 00:44:25.550
your home, we're gonna
take you somewhere tonight.

00:44:25.550 --> 00:44:28.370
And she said: no, you have
to take me back to my home.

00:44:28.370 --> 00:44:30.580
I'm gonna die in my home if I'm dying.

00:44:30.580 --> 00:44:32.510
I will die with dignity.

00:44:32.510 --> 00:44:34.560
So they brought her back to the home

00:44:34.560 --> 00:44:38.010
and had home arrest for 10 days.

00:44:38.010 --> 00:44:41.270
And what's unique, I think,
my mom is not sharing,

00:44:41.270 --> 00:44:44.640
and it's amazing, like, as a family,

00:44:44.640 --> 00:44:47.610
the whole country, we are
relieved they released her,

00:44:47.610 --> 00:44:51.290
she's home, and we want
everything, the work,

00:44:51.290 --> 00:44:52.630
to resume and everything.

00:44:52.630 --> 00:44:54.600
Let's forget about what happened.

00:44:54.600 --> 00:44:57.880
No, she insisted, she said:
no, I'm not starting anything

00:44:57.880 --> 00:44:59.773
unless they apologize.

00:45:01.070 --> 00:45:02.380
(laughing)

00:45:02.380 --> 00:45:04.108
So it's like: what the hell?

00:45:04.108 --> 00:45:06.003
(laughing)

00:45:06.003 --> 00:45:07.846
Mom, I'm begging you, this is enough.

00:45:07.846 --> 00:45:09.080
You know, you were about to get killed,

00:45:09.080 --> 00:45:11.210
and we have a lot, everything destroyed,

00:45:11.210 --> 00:45:13.995
and we have to rebuild
basically the hospital,

00:45:13.995 --> 00:45:14.840
the structures and everything.

00:45:14.840 --> 00:45:18.810
She says: no, no, they have
to write apology letter.

00:45:18.810 --> 00:45:20.523
It's not even one, three of it.

00:45:21.369 --> 00:45:22.606
(laughing)

00:45:22.606 --> 00:45:24.550
One is for me, one is for the community,

00:45:24.550 --> 00:45:26.340
and one for the MSF.

00:45:26.340 --> 00:45:28.250
That time it was Doctors Without Boarders

00:45:28.250 --> 00:45:31.210
who were running all pediatric
sections of the hospital,

00:45:31.210 --> 00:45:33.260
and they took a week conversation

00:45:33.260 --> 00:45:35.480
and back and forth and we are all like:

00:45:35.480 --> 00:45:38.220
okay, another attack can happen tomorrow.

00:45:38.220 --> 00:45:40.710
So we didn't know, but
finally we were surprised,

00:45:40.710 --> 00:45:42.040
they wrote the letter.

00:45:42.040 --> 00:45:43.400
- One letter or three?

00:45:43.400 --> 00:45:45.860
- Three letters, and they brought to her.

00:45:45.860 --> 00:45:47.820
It's like: Mama Hawa,
these are your letters.

00:45:47.820 --> 00:45:50.052
Let's open the hospital, because--

00:45:50.052 --> 00:45:52.469
(applauding)

00:45:53.438 --> 00:45:54.271
- Amazing.

00:45:56.600 --> 00:46:00.010
- It's, I really admire
her courage, I think.

00:46:00.010 --> 00:46:02.480
It's the first time in
the history of Somalia

00:46:02.480 --> 00:46:05.270
someone not only stood up to the warlords

00:46:05.270 --> 00:46:08.470
and the Islamists, but
also taught them a lesson.

00:46:08.470 --> 00:46:11.278
You need to apologize; what
you have done was wrong.

00:46:11.278 --> 00:46:12.830
(laughing)

00:46:12.830 --> 00:46:17.583
- So, since 2012-2013, much
more peace has come to Somalia.

00:46:18.815 --> 00:46:21.270
I would love to hear what
the work has been like

00:46:21.270 --> 00:46:26.160
since the peace has been more
able to stabilize the country

00:46:26.160 --> 00:46:28.930
and make the work much less dangerous

00:46:28.930 --> 00:46:32.193
than these years that
you're describing from 2010.

00:46:33.802 --> 00:46:36.000
And I believe we have some
photos of some of the work.

00:46:36.000 --> 00:46:37.849
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:46:37.849 --> 00:46:40.549
- I will share with you some
photos at the foundation.

00:46:42.030 --> 00:46:45.253
That's a beautiful photo
of Glamour, in 2010,

00:46:46.797 --> 00:46:48.640
when we received Glamour
Women of the Year.

00:46:48.640 --> 00:46:51.340
The three of us, me and
my mom and my sister.

00:46:51.340 --> 00:46:53.633
And it took two hours to take that shot.

00:46:54.627 --> 00:46:55.510
(laughing)

00:46:55.510 --> 00:46:59.660
After this photo, I have
deep respect for models.

00:46:59.660 --> 00:47:02.453
I was thinking it was very
easy to take photos before.

00:47:03.460 --> 00:47:04.843
Next, it's like: please!

00:47:05.880 --> 00:47:07.350
So that's the hospital.

00:47:07.350 --> 00:47:11.160
And this photo, I really,
it's one of my favorite photos

00:47:11.160 --> 00:47:14.100
because it's super busy,
it's the outpatient,

00:47:14.100 --> 00:47:17.420
you see the registration is going on,

00:47:17.420 --> 00:47:19.820
the PB, they're checking
the weight, you know,

00:47:19.820 --> 00:47:21.440
mothers are lining up.

00:47:21.440 --> 00:47:23.970
This is the peak, when we had over 90,000.

00:47:23.970 --> 00:47:27.820
And daily, we were receiving
500 and over patients.

00:47:27.820 --> 00:47:28.793
Next slide.

00:47:29.960 --> 00:47:32.890
So this is the medical
outreach mobile clinics

00:47:32.890 --> 00:47:34.950
we do in the village; we still do.

00:47:34.950 --> 00:47:38.370
We try to keep the, as
soon as we get some window

00:47:38.370 --> 00:47:41.727
for safety in an area to go, we go out.

00:47:41.727 --> 00:47:44.020
It's very beautiful,
it's very rewarding to go

00:47:44.020 --> 00:47:47.220
and visit and just take
all your medicine with you.

00:47:47.220 --> 00:47:50.820
There's nothing in there, and
then mothers are lining up,

00:47:50.820 --> 00:47:52.333
waiting for you; next slide.

00:47:54.193 --> 00:47:55.800
And this is the primary school.

00:47:55.800 --> 00:47:58.340
The school is very dear
to me and favored to me.

00:47:58.340 --> 00:48:01.767
It's education, I loved
it, and when my high school

00:48:01.767 --> 00:48:03.870
and my country falls apart, so,

00:48:03.870 --> 00:48:06.750
still to stick to my back,
so this school was opened

00:48:06.750 --> 00:48:10.230
in 2000, and it has a different story,

00:48:10.230 --> 00:48:12.150
where we opened and it didn't work,

00:48:12.150 --> 00:48:15.025
we opened and it closed, but since 2008

00:48:15.025 --> 00:48:17.980
and now nine years is the
school functioning fully.

00:48:17.980 --> 00:48:19.580
This is the primary school.

00:48:19.580 --> 00:48:21.700
Next slide, with my mom
in the middle of it,

00:48:21.700 --> 00:48:24.910
we took this photo when
we distributed the shirts.

00:48:24.910 --> 00:48:28.800
This is the pre-K, preschool.

00:48:28.800 --> 00:48:33.110
These toys was delivered from
Washington DC and Seattle,

00:48:33.110 --> 00:48:35.520
from Vital Voices, and
we're very grateful.

00:48:35.520 --> 00:48:40.060
So I tried my best to have as
any preschool in the world,

00:48:40.060 --> 00:48:43.610
so they can have the games
and education in early age,

00:48:43.610 --> 00:48:45.760
and we prepare for them in the future.

00:48:45.760 --> 00:48:46.683
Next slide.

00:48:48.820 --> 00:48:52.110
This is one of my favorite
photos of the primary school.

00:48:52.110 --> 00:48:52.963
Next slide.

00:48:54.850 --> 00:48:59.300
And this photo was taken by New York Times

00:48:59.300 --> 00:49:02.800
when they visited in our
primary school when it was full.

00:49:02.800 --> 00:49:07.320
Over 27,000 kids were out of the school,

00:49:07.320 --> 00:49:10.510
and we could only have in this school 800.

00:49:10.510 --> 00:49:11.960
So very small numbers.

00:49:11.960 --> 00:49:14.300
When we say 800, people
think: oh, that's a lot!

00:49:14.300 --> 00:49:18.920
But compared to the kids out
of the school, it's crazy.

00:49:18.920 --> 00:49:21.930
And what I wanna really
mention here today,

00:49:21.930 --> 00:49:24.200
this photo was taken
three, four years ago,

00:49:24.200 --> 00:49:26.610
but these kids are now in high school.

00:49:26.610 --> 00:49:29.790
And they're taking this class
that Nicola and Charlotte

00:49:29.790 --> 00:49:32.470
was talking on, Hope is Here,

00:49:32.470 --> 00:49:36.310
and all this came together
because of Sarah Luloe,

00:49:36.310 --> 00:49:39.310
and we, I want to teach,
and my mom always tells,

00:49:39.310 --> 00:49:41.190
you know, the problem
in Somalia is people,

00:49:41.190 --> 00:49:43.240
they don't know their rights.

00:49:43.240 --> 00:49:47.060
And that's her law speaking,
and that's what I decided

00:49:47.060 --> 00:49:50.630
to do and I'm really, yell
and yell for law school,

00:49:50.630 --> 00:49:55.630
and the Schell Center helped
to put this program together.

00:49:56.280 --> 00:49:57.960
And we have very big ambitions.

00:49:57.960 --> 00:49:59.760
And we were in the beginning
thinking we might just

00:49:59.760 --> 00:50:02.650
have one year, then every
year we keep adding,

00:50:02.650 --> 00:50:03.700
and we keep improving.

00:50:03.700 --> 00:50:06.225
Now we have a four-year program,

00:50:06.225 --> 00:50:08.650
and we're hoping to take this
not only in our high school,

00:50:08.650 --> 00:50:11.250
and all over Somalia and teach the kids

00:50:11.250 --> 00:50:14.210
the conflict resolution,
how this could work.

00:50:14.210 --> 00:50:15.103
Next slide.

00:50:17.090 --> 00:50:20.570
This is the map of the
village, in Google Earth.

00:50:20.570 --> 00:50:23.620
I just took it yesterday
night, so if you go look at it.

00:50:23.620 --> 00:50:26.990
I just want people to
understand how, you know,

00:50:27.860 --> 00:50:30.160
how massive it is and
how people are living in,

00:50:30.160 --> 00:50:32.790
and why we call it a village
and not a camp anymore,

00:50:32.790 --> 00:50:35.770
because people were there for 26 years,

00:50:35.770 --> 00:50:39.360
and now we have more
concrete structures in place,

00:50:39.360 --> 00:50:41.230
not only plastic sheets.

00:50:41.230 --> 00:50:45.010
And just to dignify, you
know, how they mobilize,

00:50:45.010 --> 00:50:48.200
how they organize themselves,
so this is all, ah,

00:50:48.200 --> 00:50:49.793
let me see if I can point.

00:50:51.494 --> 00:50:52.694
Is it gonna work or not?

00:50:53.820 --> 00:50:55.330
Oh yes, that's the school.

00:50:55.330 --> 00:50:57.490
That's the high school we just built.

00:50:57.490 --> 00:51:01.430
And it has a huge playground,
so the kids can have fun.

00:51:01.430 --> 00:51:03.230
So this is all small structures,

00:51:03.230 --> 00:51:06.070
the hospital, and this
is the primary school.

00:51:06.070 --> 00:51:09.180
Beginning, this is the
road, the main road.

00:51:09.180 --> 00:51:10.993
So this is the first settlement.

00:51:12.540 --> 00:51:13.980
And this is the football ground,

00:51:13.980 --> 00:51:15.460
and this is the other settlement.

00:51:15.460 --> 00:51:18.020
Each settlement we have
here, this is the back,

00:51:18.020 --> 00:51:20.520
where we have mango trees, this is the,

00:51:20.520 --> 00:51:23.370
my mom's, what she calls Camp David.

00:51:23.370 --> 00:51:25.940
She gets rest and goes back there

00:51:25.940 --> 00:51:28.960
after all the crazy work
that's going on in there.

00:51:28.960 --> 00:51:31.750
So it's very massive,
and this part of the road

00:51:31.750 --> 00:51:35.260
is our land, but we are also responsible

00:51:35.260 --> 00:51:37.240
for six kilometers around us.

00:51:37.240 --> 00:51:40.030
So the UN and international community,

00:51:40.030 --> 00:51:42.120
when there's funding
and food distribution,

00:51:42.120 --> 00:51:43.963
we cover all areas.

00:51:45.119 --> 00:51:45.952
The next slide.

00:51:47.160 --> 00:51:49.063
This is how we decide, actually.

00:51:49.981 --> 00:51:52.163
We have the committee,
and there's no office.

00:51:53.493 --> 00:51:55.172
There's no city hall you go.

00:51:55.172 --> 00:51:57.580
It's everyone comes to the Camp
David under the mango trees.

00:51:57.580 --> 00:51:59.010
So that's the mango trees.

00:51:59.010 --> 00:52:02.550
And solving a case, and we had a visitor,

00:52:02.550 --> 00:52:05.160
this young diaspora of boys and girls

00:52:05.160 --> 00:52:08.090
came to visit and see how we could solve

00:52:08.090 --> 00:52:11.290
the daily cases and
discussion between them.

00:52:11.290 --> 00:52:13.973
So I wanted to take, we
took the photo; next slide.

00:52:15.620 --> 00:52:18.750
And this is the daily new arrivals,

00:52:18.750 --> 00:52:21.990
because the, you have a
clash, people will come.

00:52:21.990 --> 00:52:24.790
The village and the camp is known

00:52:24.790 --> 00:52:27.470
all over Somalia, so
they come for temporary,

00:52:27.470 --> 00:52:30.840
when their village gets quiet,
they go back, their city.

00:52:30.840 --> 00:52:33.990
So we do have every day,
every other day, new arrivals,

00:52:33.990 --> 00:52:36.310
or every other week,
maybe two, three families.

00:52:36.310 --> 00:52:38.770
The minute they arrive, we try to register

00:52:38.770 --> 00:52:42.070
and give whatever food we
have, or plastic sheets,

00:52:42.070 --> 00:52:43.540
and find places to stay.

00:52:43.540 --> 00:52:45.620
If they have already a
relative in the village,

00:52:45.620 --> 00:52:47.390
they will stay with their relatives.

00:52:47.390 --> 00:52:48.363
Next slide.

00:52:49.760 --> 00:52:54.760
And this is another new
arrival, just waiting outside.

00:52:56.460 --> 00:52:58.430
Of the hospital, in front of the hospital.

00:52:58.430 --> 00:53:01.253
This structure, and next slide.

00:53:02.870 --> 00:53:05.940
And I wanted to just a little
bit talk about the drought.

00:53:05.940 --> 00:53:09.220
So next it's the drought, and it's, ah,

00:53:09.220 --> 00:53:10.820
it's really heartbreaking.

00:53:10.820 --> 00:53:12.920
It's all, you wanna go forward.

00:53:12.920 --> 00:53:15.290
We have a government system in place,

00:53:15.290 --> 00:53:18.124
I have been here doing my wonderful Yale

00:53:18.124 --> 00:53:20.548
(muttering) programs, six months,

00:53:20.548 --> 00:53:23.150
and then as soon as I went
home, the drought starts.

00:53:23.150 --> 00:53:26.540
The election, and I felt
like we'd gone backward

00:53:26.540 --> 00:53:30.060
instead of forward, so
it was a very tough time

00:53:30.060 --> 00:53:33.840
for me and it was very hard
to see the same drought

00:53:33.840 --> 00:53:36.260
we have seen, even worse than 2011.

00:53:36.260 --> 00:53:39.080
So 2011, the whole world supported.

00:53:39.080 --> 00:53:42.890
Somalia was there, we did
fundraising and everything,

00:53:42.890 --> 00:53:47.500
and after a short five years,
you're still in the same place

00:53:47.500 --> 00:53:49.790
and there's no structure in anything.

00:53:49.790 --> 00:53:54.300
So we gathered a lot of
young Somalis, come together,

00:53:54.300 --> 00:53:56.821
we have all our frustration, we thought:

00:53:56.821 --> 00:53:58.680
okay, let's use our
frustration for something good.

00:53:58.680 --> 00:54:03.060
So let's not wait until
international community reached us,

00:54:03.060 --> 00:54:07.580
which NGO's, everyone, we
(unintelligible) outside.

00:54:07.580 --> 00:54:09.400
Let's do something inside.

00:54:09.400 --> 00:54:12.900
And that worked, and so we
created a mobile system,

00:54:12.900 --> 00:54:16.500
we started feeding, we started
putting every 10 kilometers

00:54:16.500 --> 00:54:20.598
some feeding centers, we
gathered together information,

00:54:20.598 --> 00:54:23.000
then a dilemma comes in; it's like:

00:54:23.000 --> 00:54:24.434
we don't know who we're helping.

00:54:24.434 --> 00:54:25.780
We don't know who is where, helping.

00:54:25.780 --> 00:54:27.103
So, next slide.

00:54:29.100 --> 00:54:31.010
Oh, this is the feeding center.

00:54:31.010 --> 00:54:34.932
Now we have it in, and
this is for the children.

00:54:34.932 --> 00:54:37.321
In the beginning, we
do the porridge, cook,

00:54:37.321 --> 00:54:42.207
and all our, we took this
food from the local stores.

00:54:42.207 --> 00:54:43.040
From the business guys.

00:54:43.040 --> 00:54:45.370
So we didn't even wait until money comes.

00:54:45.370 --> 00:54:47.330
We call everyone who
knows, and they ask for,

00:54:47.330 --> 00:54:50.290
please send $100, $50, buying the food,

00:54:50.290 --> 00:54:53.551
collecting the food, high school students

00:54:53.551 --> 00:54:56.450
going out to the homes
and stores, asking food.

00:54:56.450 --> 00:54:58.510
So that's what, how we mobilize.

00:54:58.510 --> 00:54:59.363
Next slide.

00:55:01.240 --> 00:55:03.610
Next slide; this is the
children in the morning,

00:55:03.610 --> 00:55:07.660
breakfast, so, ah, it's gone, the rice.

00:55:07.660 --> 00:55:09.810
- [Liu] It might come
back; they're trying.

00:55:11.478 --> 00:55:13.628
- [Deqo] Okay, that's,
ah, so I wanted just

00:55:14.517 --> 00:55:16.267
to go back to that mob; next slide.

00:55:17.220 --> 00:55:20.730
Ah, this is the food for the
rice we cook for the adults.

00:55:20.730 --> 00:55:21.623
Next slide.

00:55:23.450 --> 00:55:24.393
And next.

00:55:25.920 --> 00:55:29.113
This is the map; I just wanna talk, what,

00:55:29.970 --> 00:55:34.270
it's the drought and
how we can reach people.

00:55:34.270 --> 00:55:36.780
Where we are reaching,
where people are dying.

00:55:36.780 --> 00:55:38.710
And as we know, when the drought comes,

00:55:38.710 --> 00:55:40.530
there's not enough water,

00:55:40.530 --> 00:55:43.200
and it's all preventable diseases comes.

00:55:43.200 --> 00:55:47.000
So immediately, like, three
weeks when I was back in Somalia

00:55:47.000 --> 00:55:51.180
the watery diarrhea broke
out, in our village.

00:55:51.180 --> 00:55:53.260
Not too far, we have enough water,

00:55:53.260 --> 00:55:56.030
but the next neighbors,
and we lost two kids.

00:55:56.030 --> 00:55:58.100
Immediately, first couple of days.

00:55:58.100 --> 00:55:59.760
And it's preventable.

00:55:59.760 --> 00:56:03.580
It's very frustrating,
being in this situation.

00:56:03.580 --> 00:56:07.670
So we created this unique map
with young Somali diaspora

00:56:07.670 --> 00:56:10.680
in Sweden, in Holland, in London,

00:56:10.680 --> 00:56:14.070
gathered it together
using technologies today,

00:56:14.070 --> 00:56:16.980
given this information, and
then you see the red area

00:56:16.980 --> 00:56:18.800
is the where we have a death.

00:56:18.800 --> 00:56:22.864
Orange, it's food that's needed,

00:56:22.864 --> 00:56:24.945
and then in green where we have NGO's.

00:56:24.945 --> 00:56:26.030
We know someone is there.

00:56:26.030 --> 00:56:29.860
And we need to call that
NGO, so we communicated

00:56:29.860 --> 00:56:33.320
within one month, we put
the whole country map.

00:56:33.320 --> 00:56:36.130
This united us; there's
no division in Somalia.

00:56:36.130 --> 00:56:39.083
We didn't care; it's that
they all show up at an area,

00:56:39.083 --> 00:56:41.950
or we had someone else, we had
to care if people were dying.

00:56:41.950 --> 00:56:45.490
And young people united
and then gathered together

00:56:45.490 --> 00:56:48.470
for only the situation in drought.

00:56:48.470 --> 00:56:51.060
And something good came out,
but it's very frustrating

00:56:51.060 --> 00:56:53.400
to me to be in that situation.

00:56:53.400 --> 00:56:57.620
And also, it's very empowering;
we could do something.

00:56:57.620 --> 00:57:02.040
We could make a difference,
before even anyone gets there

00:57:02.040 --> 00:57:04.600
and before the international
community reached us.

00:57:04.600 --> 00:57:07.300
And we reduced the numbers
of death for diarrhea,

00:57:07.300 --> 00:57:09.830
we reduced the number
of death for starvation,

00:57:09.830 --> 00:57:12.240
and this project is still going on.

00:57:12.240 --> 00:57:13.590
We have the website now,

00:57:13.590 --> 00:57:16.920
and Minister of Humanitarian Disaster

00:57:16.920 --> 00:57:20.166
is working with us to put
information in our website.

00:57:20.166 --> 00:57:23.730
Also the UN is following our website.

00:57:23.730 --> 00:57:26.250
We put the report; it's
very easy, simple thing.

00:57:26.250 --> 00:57:29.370
Everyone can go in and put
the information with the phone

00:57:29.370 --> 00:57:32.860
and the four of us will
verify: is this true or not?

00:57:32.860 --> 00:57:36.270
So we call everyone when we
have verified it's really true.

00:57:36.270 --> 00:57:39.730
We will follow and we will
ask: who's the closest one

00:57:39.730 --> 00:57:44.260
to distribute food or water
to them, or medicine, even?

00:57:44.260 --> 00:57:47.010
So that's how we mobilize,
and it's amazing,

00:57:47.010 --> 00:57:49.320
and then it's good too,
but we have to have more

00:57:49.320 --> 00:57:51.343
sustainable programs in the country.

00:57:52.503 --> 00:57:54.583
So that's, that's, ah, next slide?

00:57:55.845 --> 00:57:56.880
I think I'm done.

00:57:56.880 --> 00:58:00.610
So that's the future
generation of Somalia.

00:58:00.610 --> 00:58:03.250
Future leaders, here, and
this is one of my favorite

00:58:03.250 --> 00:58:04.663
photos in the village.

00:58:05.740 --> 00:58:07.974
- Thank you so much, this is, I think,

00:58:07.974 --> 00:58:09.530
a great place to end with thinking about

00:58:09.530 --> 00:58:11.180
all the amazing work
that you've been doing

00:58:11.180 --> 00:58:14.280
for the many decades, the
work that you're still doing,

00:58:14.280 --> 00:58:16.890
and then the hope in Somalia's future.

00:58:16.890 --> 00:58:19.815
So thank you for being here, today.

00:58:19.815 --> 00:58:22.232
(applauding)

00:58:24.495 --> 00:58:26.828
(murmuring)

00:58:33.009 --> 00:58:35.480
So, we're gonna bring the
lights back up in the house,

00:58:35.480 --> 00:58:38.770
and we will, if all of you have questions

00:58:38.770 --> 00:58:41.900
that you want to pose to
Doctors Abdi and Mohamed,

00:58:41.900 --> 00:58:44.150
we'd be very happy to have a few of them.

00:58:44.150 --> 00:58:46.600
I think we have a little
bit of time for questions.

00:58:46.600 --> 00:58:50.580
So I think everyone has,
should have a paper and pencil.

00:58:50.580 --> 00:58:52.110
So if you can pass your questions,

00:58:52.110 --> 00:58:56.712
there are aides coming
down the aisle to collect,

00:58:56.712 --> 00:58:58.500
and if you do not have a paper and pencil,

00:58:58.500 --> 00:59:00.393
just raise your hand and someone,

00:59:02.830 --> 00:59:04.700
and one of the aides should get to you.

00:59:04.700 --> 00:59:07.210
So, yeah.

00:59:07.210 --> 00:59:09.480
Mariah, I think people raising their hand

00:59:09.480 --> 00:59:11.753
if they want a paper or pencil, yeah.

00:59:16.565 --> 00:59:17.898
Alright, thanks.

00:59:22.500 --> 00:59:26.870
So we're gonna do questions
now, but thank you so much.

00:59:26.870 --> 00:59:28.070
This is wonderful, yeah.

00:59:29.814 --> 00:59:31.537
- I want to say to the audience,

00:59:31.537 --> 00:59:35.007
thank you for coming and
sharing my history with me.

00:59:37.660 --> 00:59:39.051
Yes, and I want to thank--

00:59:39.051 --> 00:59:41.468
(applauding)

00:59:43.966 --> 00:59:46.829
I want to thank also Yale University

00:59:46.829 --> 00:59:49.663
for giving me this opportunity to see,

00:59:51.116 --> 00:59:52.923
to see this beautiful college.

00:59:53.962 --> 00:59:55.491
Thank you very much.

00:59:55.491 --> 00:59:56.324
- Thank you for coming.

00:59:57.922 --> 01:00:01.589
(speaking foreign language)

01:00:11.500 --> 01:00:15.334
- Okay, Mariah, maybe we
should just bring some down.

01:00:15.334 --> 01:00:16.790
Because we can always keep collecting.

01:00:16.790 --> 01:00:19.123
Thanks so much; okay, alright.

01:00:26.090 --> 01:00:28.253
Okay, you ready for the first question?

01:00:29.234 --> 01:00:31.830
Okay, what has your experience taught you

01:00:31.830 --> 01:00:34.440
about the role of
humanitarian aid from foreign

01:00:34.440 --> 01:00:38.743
organizations and their
relationship with local NGO's?

01:00:42.663 --> 01:00:44.703
(laughing)

01:00:44.703 --> 01:00:45.870
Not an easy question.

01:00:45.870 --> 01:00:47.270
- No, it's a tough question.

01:00:48.931 --> 01:00:52.598
(speaking foreign language)

01:01:00.300 --> 01:01:02.503
Oh. (laughing)

01:01:03.770 --> 01:01:08.110
It's, humanitarian aid is
excellent in a disaster,

01:01:08.110 --> 01:01:11.670
just to deliver one time,
but when it comes alive

01:01:11.670 --> 01:01:15.403
for almost a quarter of a
century, it's a disaster.

01:01:16.320 --> 01:01:17.700
It's basically disaster.

01:01:17.700 --> 01:01:21.163
You're creating a whole
generation dependent on aid.

01:01:22.000 --> 01:01:25.780
The whole system of aid is
not set up for sustainability

01:01:25.780 --> 01:01:29.480
and for the future, so
it's all the same cycle.

01:01:29.480 --> 01:01:31.860
You fundraise, you finish the money,

01:01:31.860 --> 01:01:34.340
you come back, the project ends,

01:01:34.340 --> 01:01:37.800
and billions of money was
wasted for the last 20 years

01:01:37.800 --> 01:01:40.010
in Somalia, 25 years.

01:01:40.010 --> 01:01:44.280
And nothing is done, so
it's very painful to me.

01:01:44.280 --> 01:01:46.580
It's good to have humanitarian work,

01:01:46.580 --> 01:01:49.419
but we have to restructure, and we think

01:01:49.419 --> 01:01:52.221
in a more sustainable
way, and maybe use only

01:01:52.221 --> 01:01:55.260
what it needs for that
emergency; it's not continuously.

01:01:55.260 --> 01:01:56.373
It's not effective.

01:01:57.610 --> 01:01:59.090
- But it saves lives.

01:01:59.090 --> 01:01:59.923
- Yes.

01:02:01.960 --> 01:02:02.810
- It saves lives.

01:02:03.947 --> 01:02:06.447
- So it's a double-edged
sword, yeah, in that way.

01:02:08.693 --> 01:02:11.313
This second question,
oh, thank you so much.

01:02:12.840 --> 01:02:14.100
This,

01:02:14.100 --> 01:02:15.450
so I apologize ahead of time,

01:02:15.450 --> 01:02:16.650
because there's quite a few questions,

01:02:16.650 --> 01:02:18.570
and we'll do our best to
get through as many of them

01:02:18.570 --> 01:02:21.110
as we can, but the second question is:

01:02:21.110 --> 01:02:23.700
given your experience, you
have both been called on

01:02:23.700 --> 01:02:26.812
to join multiple peace
conferences and committees

01:02:26.812 --> 01:02:29.990
that aim to chart a peaceful
and politically sustainable

01:02:29.990 --> 01:02:31.900
future for Somalia.

01:02:31.900 --> 01:02:34.460
What has your experience
been like with these,

01:02:34.460 --> 01:02:37.250
and what can we take
away from these projects,

01:02:37.250 --> 01:02:39.250
given that many have failed in the past?

01:02:42.433 --> 01:02:46.100
(speaking foreign language)

01:02:49.350 --> 01:02:50.517
- I have no--

01:02:52.137 --> 01:02:54.970
(speaking foreign language)

01:02:54.970 --> 01:02:59.100
I know, I want to tell you
that everything you want,

01:02:59.100 --> 01:03:00.163
you can reach.

01:03:01.310 --> 01:03:05.993
Just it needs to be
persistent and committed.

01:03:07.787 --> 01:03:09.660
And nothing is impossible, this world.

01:03:09.660 --> 01:03:12.343
Although this world now is spiraling.

01:03:13.770 --> 01:03:15.623
Our world is burning, now.

01:03:18.150 --> 01:03:21.193
Greedy, terrorists, pain,

01:03:23.000 --> 01:03:26.580
and the world is ours, the world is one.

01:03:26.580 --> 01:03:28.160
Human being is one.

01:03:28.160 --> 01:03:29.563
Our needs is one.

01:03:30.860 --> 01:03:35.860
Every one of us has a
responsibility to make this world

01:03:38.050 --> 01:03:42.947
peaceful place, because
we want to live nicely.

01:03:46.020 --> 01:03:48.033
And that's dependent on us.

01:03:50.420 --> 01:03:54.663
If people, human beings know this is one,

01:03:55.860 --> 01:03:57.023
their needs, this is
one, the world is one.

01:03:58.760 --> 01:03:59.763
We are all one.

01:04:03.763 --> 01:04:06.643
- Yeah, and what'd we learn from being in,

01:04:07.770 --> 01:04:09.977
the question was, hm--

01:04:10.950 --> 01:04:11.783
- The question?

01:04:11.783 --> 01:04:13.800
- You captured, I think, some part of it.

01:04:15.244 --> 01:04:16.077
- I hope so.

01:04:16.077 --> 01:04:18.470
- Yes, you did, definitely did.

01:04:18.470 --> 01:04:20.650
I have a question that is,
in some ways, a follow-up.

01:04:20.650 --> 01:04:23.750
It says: in the face of
tribalism, war and famine,

01:04:23.750 --> 01:04:27.460
how have you found the
strength to live and love

01:04:27.460 --> 01:04:29.520
through it all, I think?

01:04:29.520 --> 01:04:31.097
What should you, ah,

01:04:33.560 --> 01:04:37.763
what would you want Somalis and
everyone to know about love?

01:04:40.761 --> 01:04:42.994
So, it's kind of a profound
question in some ways.

01:04:42.994 --> 01:04:46.661
(speaking foreign language)

01:04:50.197 --> 01:04:52.720
- God gives me that strength.

01:04:52.720 --> 01:04:54.723
- God gave you the strength, yeah.

01:04:57.154 --> 01:04:59.487
(murmuring)

01:05:01.580 --> 01:05:02.930
- You need to speak louder.

01:05:03.799 --> 01:05:05.363
- Okay, God gives me the strength.

01:05:07.810 --> 01:05:10.710
- I think, to me, it's the
people that inspire me.

01:05:10.710 --> 01:05:13.810
And I think my mom also
would say it's the people

01:05:13.810 --> 01:05:14.797
of Somalia.

01:05:14.797 --> 01:05:19.510
- The people respect me,
loves me, and I love them.

01:05:19.510 --> 01:05:21.333
I love my country and my people.

01:05:22.250 --> 01:05:26.232
I give already my life and soul

01:05:26.232 --> 01:05:27.913
for my people and my country.

01:05:28.870 --> 01:05:29.970
And they're spreading.

01:05:32.819 --> 01:05:34.550
And I'm getting also
international respect,

01:05:34.550 --> 01:05:38.373
to other human beings,
of this five continents.

01:05:41.160 --> 01:05:44.893
- Yes, you most definitely
have our deepest respect.

01:05:48.850 --> 01:05:51.620
Oh dear, it will be okay.

01:05:51.620 --> 01:05:54.930
We have a question here that
wants to have more details

01:05:54.930 --> 01:05:57.131
about the storage closet.

01:05:57.131 --> 01:05:58.250
(laughing)

01:05:58.250 --> 01:06:01.373
Is there anything more
to add to that story?

01:06:03.620 --> 01:06:05.253
- Nothing much really.

01:06:06.356 --> 01:06:11.020
It's just a way to punishing
the mistake has done.

01:06:11.020 --> 01:06:13.170
So if you let it go, what happens?

01:06:13.170 --> 01:06:15.450
And you say: talk about it.

01:06:15.450 --> 01:06:17.620
And it's not gonna be solved.

01:06:17.620 --> 01:06:19.770
But when you punish it in a soft way,

01:06:19.770 --> 01:06:23.140
because you have still a
conflict zone, you have trauma,

01:06:23.140 --> 01:06:27.590
you cannot just do as a
real jail or anything,

01:06:27.590 --> 01:06:30.698
or have strong rules and law.

01:06:30.698 --> 01:06:34.360
I think it helped a lot and it's basic,

01:06:34.360 --> 01:06:37.810
some days it's full; we don't
have space to arrest anyone.

01:06:37.810 --> 01:06:40.060
And some days the food distribution time,

01:06:40.060 --> 01:06:41.140
we don't arrest anyone.

01:06:41.140 --> 01:06:43.514
We try to find other rooms to close.

01:06:43.514 --> 01:06:47.760
That's the way it works;
nothing special or fancy.

01:06:47.760 --> 01:06:51.653
Or you don't have a specific
jail funding for that.

01:06:53.042 --> 01:06:54.104
(laughing)

01:06:54.104 --> 01:06:54.937
- Right.

01:06:54.937 --> 01:06:57.670
So this question is: what
was the most difficult

01:06:57.670 --> 01:07:02.500
disagreement or conflict that
arose in Hawa Abdi Village?

01:07:02.500 --> 01:07:03.933
That you needed to resolve?

01:07:06.270 --> 01:07:09.343
It might be difficult to
pick one, but you can try.

01:07:10.593 --> 01:07:15.020
(speaking foreign language)

01:07:15.020 --> 01:07:17.740
- To stop clan division.

01:07:17.740 --> 01:07:18.900
- Clan division?

01:07:18.900 --> 01:07:20.200
- Clan division.

01:07:20.200 --> 01:07:23.970
That was very difficult,
for all Africa and all,

01:07:23.970 --> 01:07:27.260
for Somalia, but I,

01:07:29.478 --> 01:07:34.478
I win in my village;
there is no clan division.

01:07:34.840 --> 01:07:37.770
But all other country in Africa,

01:07:37.770 --> 01:07:40.603
they have clan division, even Somali more.

01:07:43.250 --> 01:07:47.410
- It's, what I think we always
have the conversation is,

01:07:47.410 --> 01:07:52.100
the problem in how we
are very linked and tied

01:07:52.100 --> 01:07:55.060
and dependent on clan
because it's insurance.

01:07:55.060 --> 01:07:59.350
Basically, when you have a
sick child or there's a death,

01:07:59.350 --> 01:08:01.120
your clan funds you.

01:08:01.120 --> 01:08:04.050
So that's why people are
dependent on clan issues.

01:08:04.050 --> 01:08:06.180
So I think the biggest issue she solved

01:08:06.180 --> 01:08:09.740
is to release this dependency to the clan.

01:08:09.740 --> 01:08:11.930
So when people have the opportunity,

01:08:11.930 --> 01:08:13.810
the hospital where they
can bring their kids

01:08:13.810 --> 01:08:15.490
when they're sick, you know, the community

01:08:15.490 --> 01:08:17.970
that supports them when they need things,

01:08:17.970 --> 01:08:21.340
I think the biggest problem
she solved, she's not saying,

01:08:21.340 --> 01:08:25.210
is just making, teaching
people you don't have to be,

01:08:25.210 --> 01:08:28.050
belong to clan allegiance
and agree with them,

01:08:28.050 --> 01:08:32.110
wrong and right; you can
be independent and have,

01:08:32.110 --> 01:08:35.250
as a human being, and
support your fellow humans.

01:08:35.250 --> 01:08:37.190
I think that was the
biggest challenge I think

01:08:37.190 --> 01:08:39.210
she solved in the camp.

01:08:39.210 --> 01:08:43.031
But the daily cases, one by
one, it happens, you know?

01:08:43.031 --> 01:08:43.864
It's a war zone.

01:08:43.864 --> 01:08:46.280
Today you have a gun pointed on you,

01:08:46.280 --> 01:08:49.783
and her kidnap situation to
me was the most hard part.

01:08:52.540 --> 01:08:54.903
- So, I think I'm gonna conclude by,

01:08:55.979 --> 01:08:57.290
there are two quick comments.

01:08:57.290 --> 01:09:00.140
I believe we might have
some Somalis in the audience

01:09:00.140 --> 01:09:03.270
who wanna express their
thanks to you for all

01:09:03.270 --> 01:09:04.730
the amazing work that you've done.

01:09:04.730 --> 01:09:07.760
So I just wanna make
sure to acknowledge that,

01:09:07.760 --> 01:09:08.593
that voice of gratitude.

01:09:08.593 --> 01:09:10.570
And then the last question is:

01:09:10.570 --> 01:09:12.423
how can we best support your work?

01:09:13.361 --> 01:09:15.088
- Oh, fantastic.

01:09:15.088 --> 01:09:16.421
(laughing)

01:09:16.421 --> 01:09:17.254
In many, many ways.

01:09:17.254 --> 01:09:19.654
You can join the wonderful Two Program,

01:09:19.654 --> 01:09:22.220
this is going on in Yale, at
the business class in Jackson,

01:09:22.220 --> 01:09:25.700
supporting the ideas, and one of the,

01:09:25.700 --> 01:09:29.517
really what I needed, my doing here,

01:09:29.517 --> 01:09:31.670
and we talk a lot with
my mom and my sister,

01:09:31.670 --> 01:09:33.570
how we could be sustainable.

01:09:33.570 --> 01:09:36.510
How we could be independent,
how we could create more jobs.

01:09:36.510 --> 01:09:40.870
That brings the peace and
stability in the country.

01:09:40.870 --> 01:09:43.030
And we have a great opportunity here,

01:09:43.030 --> 01:09:46.410
at Jackson Institute, to
do, are helping us to create

01:09:46.410 --> 01:09:49.370
a business in a failed state,
which is one of its kind

01:09:49.370 --> 01:09:51.570
in the world, I think
so, and we're trying.

01:09:51.570 --> 01:09:53.700
So you can get involved physically,

01:09:53.700 --> 01:09:57.350
or it might be the school,
for the education program,

01:09:57.350 --> 01:10:01.453
or the best thing is to give
money, go to the website.

01:10:02.430 --> 01:10:04.340
So this is one thing I've learned.

01:10:04.340 --> 01:10:06.340
You cannot do anything without money.

01:10:06.340 --> 01:10:07.960
That's how we're creating the business.

01:10:07.960 --> 01:10:10.740
We have to be very honest to ourselves.

01:10:10.740 --> 01:10:13.280
The majority why these states are failing

01:10:13.280 --> 01:10:15.480
is because there's no funding;
the government is weak

01:10:15.480 --> 01:10:18.420
and corrupted and people
don't have their money.

01:10:18.420 --> 01:10:20.900
So when you don't have a job,

01:10:20.900 --> 01:10:22.973
you will not be able to do anything.

01:10:24.920 --> 01:10:26.810
- Thank you so much, and of course,

01:10:26.810 --> 01:10:28.610
we will let you have the last words.

01:10:28.610 --> 01:10:31.140
So whatever last, parting comments,

01:10:31.140 --> 01:10:34.040
you have all the audience,
please feel free to share them.

01:10:34.935 --> 01:10:38.810
(speaking foreign language)

01:10:38.810 --> 01:10:43.500
- I tell all human beings they are one.

01:10:43.500 --> 01:10:46.040
And they will have to defend each other.

01:10:46.040 --> 01:10:48.780
We have to defend each other.

01:10:48.780 --> 01:10:52.343
We have to try to live a
peaceful and good life,

01:10:53.270 --> 01:10:57.563
and that is, we can do, we can do that.

01:10:58.530 --> 01:11:03.530
If we know reality, that
we are one, everywhere.

01:11:04.730 --> 01:11:07.670
When something happens in
one corner of this world,

01:11:07.670 --> 01:11:10.153
the other corner will split and reach.

01:11:11.310 --> 01:11:15.383
So we have to stop every bad
thing and help each other.

01:11:17.260 --> 01:11:21.823
I want to say that people
have to support each other,

01:11:22.660 --> 01:11:27.084
have to prevent every
bad thing in this world,

01:11:27.084 --> 01:11:29.890
and make this world a
better place for living.

01:11:32.365 --> 01:11:35.723
For us, for our children,
for our grandchildren.

01:11:38.039 --> 01:11:39.631
- Thank you so much.

01:11:39.631 --> 01:11:40.625
- Thank you.

01:11:40.625 --> 01:11:43.042
(applauding)

01:11:57.885 --> 01:12:00.552
(musical theme)

